WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.520
- Well, welcome everybody to the first

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.870
of two Franke Visiting Fellows Lectures,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:17.180
our opportunity to share with
the larger Yale Community,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.030
Work in Progress by James McAuley tonight

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:26.030
and by Alejandra Oliva
on Thursday, April 21st.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.930
the Franke Visiting
Fellows serve as catalysts

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.630
for intellectual gatherings at the

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.610
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.430
setting their own agendas

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.460
and presenting their
work to the community.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.970
Their presence ensues
ongoing interdisciplinary

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.800
exchange and creative debate at the

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.470
Whitney in particular
and at Yale in general.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.350
Franke Visiting Fellows
are an integral part

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.600
of the Whitney Humanities Center Fellowship.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.430
And this year I've been
especially grateful

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.010
to James McAuley and Alejandra Oliva

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.630
for their supportive presence

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.713
in our Wednesday fellows forum.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.110
The Franke Visiting
Fellows Program is made

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.350
possible by the generosity
of Richard and Barbara,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.210
Franke, who have done so much for

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.510
the Whitney Humanity Center.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.270
Tonight, I'm delighted to
introduce James McAuley,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.573
whom we've come to know as Jake.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.080
He graduated from Harvard with a degree

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.893
in History and Literature.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.740
Awarded a Marshall Scholarship
in his senior year,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.910
he went on to complete a PhD

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:34.730
in History at Oxford University

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.210
then served as Paris Correspondent
for the Washington Post

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.323
while he continued his
research in French history.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.674
In 2021,

00:01:43.674 --> 00:01:47.300
Jake published the
House of Fragile Things,

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.250
Jewish Art collectors
in the Fall of France,

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.710
just now awarded the
National Jewish Book Award.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.540
It was all over Twitter.
(audience clapping)

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.630
Last night.
(laughing)

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.500
This gripping book,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.410
really, gripping book takes us into

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.980
the world of the great
Jewish art collectors

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.090
of 20th century France,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.210
describing their contribution
to a French nation

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:15.140
that ultimately betrayed
and destroyed them.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.320
Next, Jake will publish a biography

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.410
of the Zion's philanthropist
Edmond Rothschild

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.173
in Yale University Press'
Jewish Live Series.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.280
The work Jake has been doing here

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.850
at the Whitney widens his lens.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.530
Black milk of Dawn will be an overarching

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.680
history of Holocaust memory reconsidered

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.940
at this crucial moment when
the last witnesses are dying.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.570
he will show that there was never anything

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:47.960
automatic about naming or
remembering the Holocaust

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:51.810
and that denial and
revisionism have dogged

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.060
the efforts of historians
from the beginning.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.440
This is a courageous
project for he's willing

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.780
to look at the cracks in the edifice of

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.780
Holocaust memory and to
interrogate its possible futures.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.640
I should add that Jake
is one of my favorite

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.300
opinion writers,
( Jake laughing)

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.470
whether he's writing about the Demise of

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:15.490
the French Left in the
New York review of books,

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.820
calling Putin on his distortions in

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.570
the Washington Post or challenging

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:24.660
Alan Finkle Crowd on
cancel culture hysteria

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.080
on France Culture Radio.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.390
Jake's exemplary engagement as historian

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.233
and critic shows that
understanding the past

00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:36.310
can contribute to being a positive agent

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.080
for change in one's own time.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.939
Please join me in welcoming Jake McCauley.

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:44.939
(audience clapping)

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.360
- Ah, well, thank you so much, Alice

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.000
for that very kind introduction.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.590
And indeed for having
me here at the Whitney,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.230
it has been just incredible
opportunity to read

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:06.110
and think and reflect these
past couple of months.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.680
And I'm afraid you're
gonna have to prime me

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.948
out of the office when the clock runs out.

00:04:10.948 --> 00:04:14.470
and thank you to all of our audience

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.682
members for joining tonight.

00:04:16.682 --> 00:04:19.110
I really appreciate that.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.720
And it has been just a pleasure more

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.220
than I can say to be here

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.091
and to begin the research for my next book

00:04:27.091 --> 00:04:29.340
which Alice mentioned briefly

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.900
and which is tentatively titled

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.463
Black Milk of Dawn Jewish
Survival and Holocaust Memory.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:40.130
I'm gonna talk with you
today about Salo Baron,

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.830
one of the principle
characters in that story,

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:44.540
a man whose personal journey

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.264
I find immensely inspiring

00:04:46.264 --> 00:04:50.150
and whose historical
writings I find compelling,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.740
confusing and from time
to time frustrating,

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.110
sort of all at once.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.680
And I should say that, you know,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.910
I have my background in French history

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.980
and the are sort of new readings for me.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.380
And I'm well aware that
many of our esteemed guests

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.430
in the audience tonight
are far more well versed

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.240
than I am in Baron and all of his work.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.300
So, please let me know
if I get anything wrong

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.300
and I welcome any feedback
you may have at the end.

00:05:20.365 --> 00:05:22.080
But first of all,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.420
a brief bit of biography
on Baron whose student

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.735
Yosef Hayim later called
him the greatest...

00:05:28.735 --> 00:05:32.260
Sorry, this is Baron himself,

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.100
whose students Yosef
Hayim later called him

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.870
the greatest Jewish historian
of the 20th century.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.550
A title I don't think
anyone has ever disputed.

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.820
And that I myself will certainly
not be disputing tonight.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.480
Indeed Baron who spent
his entire career laboring

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.810
over his 18 volume social
and religious history

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.640
of the Jews was the 20th Century's answer

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.220
to the likes of Simon
Dubnow and Henrich Graetz

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.800
from the 19th century.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.739
Like Dubnows, Velt
Gistictor and (indistinct)

00:06:00.739 --> 00:06:04.270
Baron's social and religious history

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:06.390
was an attempt by one man to write

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.980
the entire course of Jewish history,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.730
a monumental task if ever there were one.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.010
And this was truly his life's work.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:16.290
He began the project as a young scholar

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.810
and constantly expanding the volume until

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.933
just before his death in 1989.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.375
And these of course are Baron's,

00:06:27.375 --> 00:06:29.217
Antacede, Greats and Dubnow.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.950
So to continue,

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.120
Baron was born into a wealthy family

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.240
in Tarno Alisia, which
is in present day Poland.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.000
His father was a banker
and was also the president

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.510
of the town's Jewish community.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.060
And it was a population of roughly 16,000

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:50.053
according to available
estimates that we have.

00:06:50.053 --> 00:06:51.360
By all accounts,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.650
the young Baron was already a polymath.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:56.200
In keeping with the style
of local Jewish elites

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.040
at the time he could
say the Polish, Yiddish,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.780
biblical and modern
Hebrew, French and German

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.950
and his students among whom ranked some

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:07.060
of the greatest Jewish
historians of the 20th century,

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.860
Yori Shalmy whom I've already mentioned,

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.719
but also Arthur Hertzberg,
the list goes on and on,

00:07:12.719 --> 00:07:15.310
Would recall his ability to lecture

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.670
without notes and at least five languages.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.960
And here I am lecturing with notes in

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:21.160
my own native language.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.050
So, I mean, it's quite impressive.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:25.680
In any case Baron received rabbinical

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.900
ordination at the Jewish
Theological Seminary

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.810
in Vienna in the early 1920s

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.070
and earned no fewer than three doctorates

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.410
from the University of Vienna all by 1923.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.100
He began his teaching career at

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.447
the Jewish Teachers
College in Vienna whereby

00:07:40.447 --> 00:07:42.800
Stepen Wise persuaded
him to move to New York

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.580
to teach at the Jewish
Institute of Religion there,

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:47.740
which he did by the end of the decade.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.860
And of course it was there in New York

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.800
that he was poached by Columbia University

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.470
a few short years later,

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.080
where he became the first
to teach Jewish history

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.730
as a historical discipline
in the American University.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.720
And there's been much written about that

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.750
including a recent volume
by many leading scholars

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.830
in the field celebrating his contribution

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:09.870
to Jewish studies in the
American University specifically.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.300
But just a little bit more about

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.240
his attachment to Columbia.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:14.800
In May 1928,

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.710
Linda Miller of New Rochelle New York sent

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.790
a letter to Nicholas Butler who was then

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:21.541
the president of Columbia,

00:08:21.541 --> 00:08:23.520
informing him of her desire to establish

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.440
an endowed chair in Jewish history

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.610
and honor of her late husband who had died

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:29.850
about six months before.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:30.966
And by the way,

00:08:30.966 --> 00:08:32.300
this is a picture from
the Columbia archives

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:33.580
of the University in the twenties,

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.352
sort of around the time
when he would've arrived.

00:08:36.352 --> 00:08:39.460
And all the letters between Linda Miller

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.450
and Butler in the Columbia archives

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:42.980
and Miller's vision for this

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.810
new teaching role is
quite revealing, I think.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.420
And I quote, "I should
like to make it clear

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.980
that it is the spiritual
and intellectual aspects

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:52.540
of Jewish life that I should hope to

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:55.170
see accentuated in the courses rather

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.480
than the nationalistic
ideas which have recently

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.520
become popular in some quarters."

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:01.860
She wrote to the University President.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.800
Eventually the search landed on Baron,

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.370
although not without its
share of drama as usual.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:09.330
And he took up his post
in 1930 and he was on

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.823
the upper west side until
his retirement in 1963.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:17.870
By the time he arrived
at Columbia, of course,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.640
Baron was already well
known for an article

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.380
that is still an essential reference

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.310
for all students of Jewish studies.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.330
And which sadly seems to
be the only piece of his

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:29.730
work that is still widely read today.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.300
This as many of you may guess,

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.070
is the essay entitled
Ghetto in Emancipation,

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:38.104
his famous 1928 argument
against the so-called

00:09:38.104 --> 00:09:41.440
Laquamores conception
of Jewish history before

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.060
emancipation in the French revolution.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.880
This was a hugely
influential intervention.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.850
And it's this argument that would inform

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.053
the rest of his long and prolific career.

00:09:51.053 --> 00:09:54.310
And I quote, "Surely Baron
wrote in the last line

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:56.300
of that piece it is time break with the

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:59.170
Laquamore Theory of Pre Revolutionary War

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:02.930
and to adopt a view more in a
accord with historical truth."

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:04.770
I should also note that in that volume,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.560
I mentioned recently published in

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.200
honor of Baron's legacy,

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.350
there's a masterful
examination of that essay

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.353
and its themes by none other
than Yale's own David Sorkin.

00:10:16.187 --> 00:10:17.600
But in general,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.350
Baron was skeptical of the view

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:23.480
that saw emancipation as the solution

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.010
to the Jewish problem in Europe.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.210
And he argued Contra,
Graetz and others that

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:28.498
and I quote,

00:10:28.498 --> 00:10:29.810
" There was no complete contrast between

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:32.360
the black of the middle ages and the white

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.660
of the post emancipation period."

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.790
What mattered for Baron
was that Jewish history

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:40.180
was not to be understood
as a teleology of tragedy

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.853
or as a saga of suffering.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.610
But Baron could scarcely have imagined

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:49.660
or anticipated the brutal
century into which he was born.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:51.660
One that was certainly the most Laquamore

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.243
of any in the entire Jewish experience.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.880
And this is by the way,

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:01.620
a photograph of Baron's
hometown Tarno from

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.792
the US Holocaust Memorial with the...

00:11:03.792 --> 00:11:07.500
It captures sort of
(indistinct) persecutions

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.232
of the actual community that he came from.

00:11:10.232 --> 00:11:13.410
So, although he had left
Europe years before,

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.400
the Holocaust was more than a rupture in

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:18.320
his understanding of Jewish
history and its evolution.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.760
It was a deeply personal rupture as well.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.910
And this I think is a
significantly underappreciated

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.700
aspect of his own story and scholarship.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.630
Most of his family that
had remained in Europe,

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.650
including his parents and
his sister were murdered.

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:35.370
but even still he continued
with his argument against

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:38.180
the Laquamore theory
insisting that Jewish history

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.200
could not be allowed to become a history

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:41.830
of persecution alone,

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:43.820
but that it was a history
of the intellectual,

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.730
cultural and even spiritual responses that

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:48.430
the Jews had faced as a people

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:50.270
throughout their long history.

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:52.110
Baron found solace in the promise

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:53.970
and the reality of Jewish survival,

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:56.280
at least as he understood it.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.380
in my reading so far,

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.970
and there are so many of them
when it comes to Salo Baron.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.560
Among the most poignant
has been the conclusion

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.700
of volume three of Baron's first draft

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.260
of a social and religious
history of the Jews.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:14.260
Which was published by Columbia
university Press in 1937.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.130
So, after Hitler's rise to power,

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.480
after the Nuremberg Laws but before

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.570
the full extent of Nazi
barbarity was clear.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:23.490
At the end of the shorter although still

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.570
three volume version of the book

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:28.630
that would later become
Baron's Magnum Opus,

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:30.350
he acknowledges the concern.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.710
He correctly assumed would
be on his reader's minds.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:35.450
Would the Jewish people withstand

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:38.440
the Nazi menace gaining
traction by the day?

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.210
He was adamant in his response.

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:42.360
And he insisted that
the answer was already

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.150
clear in the course of the history he

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.040
had told in the approximately thousand

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.390
pages up to that point.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:49.377
And here I quote,

00:12:49.377 --> 00:12:51.260
"The answer to the searching question

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.130
of our time is evidently given."

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:54.057
He wrote,

00:12:54.057 --> 00:12:56.040
"The Jewish people and
their religion are going

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.580
to survive the present
extraordinary crisis."

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.563
And he under underscored are going.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:04.380
He quoted the book of Isaiah

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.377
and called on Jewish leaders to quote,

00:13:06.377 --> 00:13:09.554
"Sing unto the Lord, a
new song, a song that

00:13:09.554 --> 00:13:12.820
rebuilt Jewish life through culture

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:16.320
and education as a value
worthy of affirmation."

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:18.500
But this was before the final solution,

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.820
a catastrophe that would
touch Baron personally

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:23.270
and professionally
because the man who argued

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:25.320
so powerfully that Jewish history

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.240
itself was a form of
solace and a blueprint

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.260
for regeneration in of itself was suddenly

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:33.530
confronted with the question of memory.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.340
specifically what to
remember about the Holocaust,

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:39.670
which in Baron's time did
not yet even have a name.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:42.190
How much weight was
this unspeakable event?

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:45.280
Which Baron initially
called the Great Catastrophe

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:47.770
to be given in the long
degree of Jewish history.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:50.127
The likes of which he wrote over

00:13:50.127 --> 00:13:51.480
the course of his entire career.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:52.990
And most importantly,

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:55.320
did it represent the reputation of

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:58.783
the Anti Laquamore
convictions once and for all.

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:00.850
In my talk today,

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:02.210
I'm going to examine Baron,

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:04.200
not so much as his historian

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.730
but as a historical actor himself.

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:08.450
As someone involved intimately with

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:11.670
the construction of Holocaust
memory in two key moments.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:14.130
First in Offenbach after the war with

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.500
the Jewish Cultural Reconstruction Project

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.370
and then at the Eichmann Trial,

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:21.110
where he was a star witness
for the prosecution.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.960
In Zahor, the classic 1982 treaties

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.150
on Jewish History versus
Jewish Memory written

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:28.460
by Baron's student Yori Shalmy.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:31.560
We are asked the following
question in the introduction,

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.337
and here I quote Yori Shalmy,

00:14:33.337 --> 00:14:36.820
"But what were the Jews to
remember and by what means?

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:39.980
Baron himself had his answers and his life

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.707
and his career were a
sort of explanation."

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.350
So to begin with Offenbach after the war,

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.850
before the end of the Second World War,

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.290
the American Jewish Conference
published a pamphlet

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:57.110
entitled Program for Post-War
Jewish Reconstruction,

00:14:57.110 --> 00:14:59.550
calling for an end to antisemitism,

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.860
aid for surviving European Jews,

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.000
a war crimes tribunal

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.170
and restitution of stolen property.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:08.230
It was Baron who wrote this pamphlet

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:09.890
and it was significant that he used

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:11.540
the word reconstruction.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:12.750
This was very much the view

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:15.360
of American Jewish
Organizations in those days,

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:19.440
unable to process in the
sort of minor detail,

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.785
the unfolding disaster
in its full totality

00:15:21.785 --> 00:15:23.170
and therefore,

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:25.720
focusing on the on what was to come after

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:27.933
the war which had yet to arrive.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:32.000
Baron was seemingly incapable at that time

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:35.250
of grasping the extent of
the ongoing persecutions

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.890
in Europe, not to mention
their intensification.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.210
Like many other Jewish
scholars of his time

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:41.880
especially in the United States

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.450
he assumed that the Jews
would survive Nazism

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:46.210
as they had survived other pogroms

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.020
and expulsions in the past.

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:50.260
In 1940 Baron wrote the following,

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:51.900
Reflecting on the Nature of Europe's

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:55.540
Multinational Empires that he so valued.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:57.705
It has been an old historic and this is

00:15:57.705 --> 00:15:58.743
(mumbles)

00:15:58.743 --> 00:15:59.576
And I quote,

00:15:59.576 --> 00:16:01.570
"It has been an old historic experience

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:03.250
that the Jews suffered more heavily

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:04.840
and purely national states than

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:07.030
in countries of multiple nationality.

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.550
Germany's nationalistic
spirit draws the country

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:12.060
irresistibly into military adventures.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.710
Should it win and conquer
large territories,

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:16.980
it would lose its national homogeneity

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:19.870
and become a state of
multiple nationality which

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:23.850
incidentally might cool
its anti semitic zest."

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.593
So that's Baron in 1940.

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:30.720
Eventually Baronn and
others confronted were

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:32.750
confronted with the
extent of the destruction

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.180
and the devastation including the loss

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.320
of his two parents and one of his sisters.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.210
He would later recall that turnoff,

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:42.140
which had about 20,000 Jews before the war

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:46.040
had only about 20 when he
next visited in the 1950s.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:49.530
And yet despite his
immense personal trauma

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:50.940
and profound loss,

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:53.050
he refused to alter his view of either

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:55.820
the Jewish past and certainly
not the Jewish present.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:59.300
He threw himself into aiding
survivors however he could,

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.830
but as an architect of postwar memory

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.690
his view seems to have been...

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:06.450
Or one of the architects I
should say of postwar memory.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:08.850
His view seems to have
been that what was most

00:17:09.837 --> 00:17:11.240
important to remember was the richness

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:13.560
of the civilization that once was.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:17.400
Not so much the evil mechanics
of the destruction itself.

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.100
Baron soon headed what was called

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:21.610
the Jewish Cultural Reconstruction,

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:23.150
which was a task force sponsored by

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:26.090
the US government in
April, 1947 to collect

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:28.374
and distribute airless Jewish property in

00:17:28.374 --> 00:17:30.250
the American occupied zone

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.810
of Germany after World War II.

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:33.970
So based in Offenbach.

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:35.920
Those who worked with
Baron in that project

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.600
were fellow future arbiter
of Holocaust Memory.

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:39.980
Not all of whom agreed with him.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.640
I I should note, Hannah Arendt especially

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:44.200
but also Lucy Dawidowicz

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:46.160
and Gershom Scholem were all in some ways

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:49.390
involved with this postwar
restitution project.

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:54.390
So the archives in
Washington DC have many of

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:58.360
the materials pertaining
to this task force.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:02.630
This is one of them
here is Hannah Arendt .

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.760
One of Baron's sort of
his deputy and colleagues

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:08.800
in that major undertaking

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:13.453
And here is Lucy Dawidowicz
as a young student.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:16.913
Let's see.

00:18:18.891 --> 00:18:22.560
Through Jewish Cultural Reconstruction,

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:24.840
Baron was building a Holocaust Memory

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.040
that was based on his image of the Jewish

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:28.470
people's enduring strength

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.830
and above all else will to survive.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:33.180
He by no means ignored the extent

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:34.870
of the catastrophe in Europe.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.750
A catastrophe that had as we've mentioned,

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.000
had claimed members of his own family,

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.260
but he was nevertheless
invested in designing

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:46.009
and advocating a means of
continuation by whatever means.

00:18:46.009 --> 00:18:48.690
And here I turned to the historian,

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:50.090
Barbara Kirsten, black gimblet,

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:52.920
who has written that Baron insisted

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:55.000
that there were two essential elements

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.570
in what she calls Jewish continuity.

00:18:57.570 --> 00:19:00.570
That is first, maintained
commitment to tradition.

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:04.450
And second, the enduring
ties of Jewish community.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:07.955
So in 1945, before the war had even ended,

00:19:07.955 --> 00:19:11.860
Baron was still expressing
these hopes however

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:13.600
fanciable they may seem to us now.

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:14.587
And here I quote,

00:19:14.587 --> 00:19:16.930
"Religious history has given us evidence

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:19.120
of how deeply humanity may sink

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.440
and still rise again to
high spiritual levels.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.630
It is not unreasonable to hope that out of

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:26.540
the depths of misery and despair,

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.750
some new saving forms of belief

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:31.240
and observance may
arise which will provide

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:32.930
an answer to the perplexities

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.860
of our Jewish experience."

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:37.310
As the historian
Elizabeth Goluch has noted

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:38.940
in her brilliant recent study,

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:40.740
A Mortuary of Books,

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:43.020
Baron's general idea was at salvaging

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:46.470
the material remains of
the Jewish civilization

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:49.890
that had been, would
keep future generations

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:52.160
building on those traditions of the past

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:55.070
and furthering Jewish
knowledge in the present.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:56.670
as Goluch writes,

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:59.500
his efforts to salvage
cultural property fit with

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:02.340
the notion in every respect symbolizing

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:05.900
the potential to secure
and preserve past worlds,

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:07.733
their knowledge and traditions.

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:11.546
When he was asked to sum
up the work of the JCR,

00:20:11.546 --> 00:20:14.812
this organization in 1955,

00:20:14.812 --> 00:20:17.920
Baron underscored all of those themes.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:18.753
And he wrote,

00:20:18.753 --> 00:20:21.050
"The Jewish people succeeded in salvaging

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:24.210
500,000 volumes, 1200 scrolls of law,

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.830
7,000 artistic and ceremonial objects."

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:29.460
He wrote. "Far beyond
their monetary value.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.180
These collections symbolize the continuity

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.330
of the heritage of the Jewish people."

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.620
In a letter he sent to
the State Department,

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.850
the objects he and his
team were able to recover,

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:42.150
especially the books were
the material manifestations

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.690
of the legacy he sought to reconstruct.

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:46.430
He wrote that books have always been

00:20:46.430 --> 00:20:49.430
the very lifeblood of
the people of the book.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:51.660
Which shows that for him books were a kind

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:54.680
of representation of the
Jewish people as a whole.

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:57.140
To save their orphan
books was thus to preserve

00:20:57.140 --> 00:20:58.710
what mattered most about the Jews

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:00.510
as a civilization and a culture.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:03.170
And this, as you can see,

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:06.310
this is a photo of some
of the books that were

00:21:06.310 --> 00:21:09.293
recovered in that sort
of Offenbach Initiative.

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:13.760
And now to look at the
Eichmann Trial a little bit

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:16.280
and Baron's testimony at that moment.

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:17.730
So the next moment,

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.190
when he was a major actor in the emergence

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.040
of Holocaust memory was the Eichmann Trial,

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:24.280
which needs no introduction.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.340
It's unclear if Baron ever knew he wasn't

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.530
the Israeli government's first choice as

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:31.510
the prosecution star witness.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:33.450
They had wanted the Historian and Juris,

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:36.180
Jacob Robinson who served
as a Special Advisor

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:39.660
on Jewish affairs at the
Nuremberg trials to Israel

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:42.570
and also to Israel at the United Nations.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:44.910
Most famously Robinson would write

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:47.472
the Blistering Rebuttal to Hannah Arendt,

00:21:47.472 --> 00:21:48.910
Eichmann in Jerusalem,

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:49.790
a book entitled,

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:51.900
And the Crooked Shall be Made Straight.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:54.720
But the Israeli consulate
in New York reached Baron

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:56.480
on the upper west side regardless,

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.880
inviting him to participate
in what was certainly

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:01.040
among the most important events in

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.340
the postwar attempt to establish

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:05.990
a durable memory of the Holocaust.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:07.880
The significance of the Eichmann Trial

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:09.570
in the history of Holocaust memory

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.320
is impossible to understate.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:15.360
The dramatic arrest and
extradition of Adolph Eichmann

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:17.530
from Argentina sedated and dressed

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:20.900
in an LL flight attendants
uniform now captured

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:23.970
in various popular films
and novels that I'm sure

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:25.394
many of us have seen,

00:22:25.394 --> 00:22:28.120
was not just an attempt by the young state

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:30.650
of Israel to establish
itself on the world stage.

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:32.560
Although it was certainly
a watershed moment

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.120
in the country's history.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:36.550
In the words of the historian Tom Segev,

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.510
once Ben Gurion solemnly announced that

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:40.610
the government had Eichmann in custody.

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:42.134
And I quote,

00:22:42.134 --> 00:22:43.750
"Israelis had not known
since the declaration

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:47.270
of independence, so deep a
sense of national unity."

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:48.960
But the trial was more than a mural

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:50.760
illustration of national strength.

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:53.350
It was a deliberate
attempt to show the world

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:56.210
just what had befallen the
Jewish people as a whole

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:58.700
little more than a decade before.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:01.200
And here I quote the
famous first paragraph

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:03.230
of the Chief Prosecutor, Gideon Hausner,

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.773
who is on the left right there.

00:23:05.773 --> 00:23:09.260
He gave a 10 hour speech at the trial.

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:11.910
And this is the first paragraph of

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:15.530
his kind of quite amazing speech.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:19.070
"When I stand before you
hear judges of Israel

00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:21.320
to lead the prosecution of Adolph Eichmann,

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:23.330
I am not standing alone.

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:25.680
With me are 6 million accusers,

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.750
but they cannot rise to their feet

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.920
and point an accusing finger towards him

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:31.840
who sits in the dock and cry.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:35.050
I accuse for their ashes are piled up high

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:37.770
on the Hills of Auschwitz
and the fields of Treblinka,

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:40.490
and are strew in the forest of Poland.

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:42.430
Their graves are scattered
throughout the length

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:43.520
and breadth of Europe.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:44.720
Their blood cries out

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:46.600
but their voice is not heard.

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:49.020
Therefore I will be their
spokesman and in their

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:52.156
name I will unfold the
terrible indictment.

00:23:52.156 --> 00:23:55.780
Visibility was paramount
to that indictment.

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:58.250
Journalists from around the
world covered the trial.

00:23:58.250 --> 00:23:59.780
As we all know, Hannah Arendt

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.160
but also Ellie Wiesel

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:03.930
and Hugh Trevor-Roper
from Britain among others.

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:06.460
Most importantly, as many of you know,

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:08.580
this was the first trial
ever to be recorded

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:10.900
for television as only select portions

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:13.430
of the postwar Nuremberg
trials were filmed.

00:24:13.430 --> 00:24:15.050
But a television network recorded

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.170
the daily proceedings
which were then flown out

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:19.710
every night to the Europe and the US,

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:22.480
quite the undertaking for 1961.

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:24.590
But this was the point
of the entire exercise

00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:26.930
as Gideon Hausner would later recall.

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:29.230
The idea was that the
whole world could watch.

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:32.230
Baron took his assignment of being

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:34.310
the historical consciousness of these

00:24:34.310 --> 00:24:39.130
6 million absent accusers
extremely seriously.

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:39.963
His student,

00:24:39.963 --> 00:24:42.750
the great historian in
Rabbi Arthur Hertzberg

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:44.900
later recalled that he had never seen

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:47.050
his professor as anxious as he was during

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:49.144
this final preparations for the trial.

00:24:49.144 --> 00:24:52.169
And the Israeli government
had made his assignment clear.

00:24:52.169 --> 00:24:55.134
In order to convict Eichmann forcefully,

00:24:55.134 --> 00:24:57.730
in order to demonstrate the full extent

00:24:57.730 --> 00:24:59.850
of his brutality and his evil,

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:02.260
the prosecution needed
to show the significance

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:03.670
of what the Nazi's had done

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:06.790
and just what exactly they had destroyed.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:08.080
In advance of the trial,

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:10.550
Hausner and this I borrow
from Deborah Lipstadt's

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:11.860
book on the trial,

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:14.260
Hausner had written to
Baron that it was important

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:15.680
to prove the Nazi intention

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:17.560
to annihilate the Jewish people.

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:19.530
And it is therefore vital for the trial

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:21.891
to present documentation that will expose

00:25:21.891 --> 00:25:25.490
the national and cultural
value of the Jewish Centers

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:27.353
that were destroyed and Holocaust.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:32.220
When Baron arrived in
Israel in late March, 1961,

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:34.940
he had an audience with none
other than Ben Gurion himself.

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:37.187
And of course that is Ben Gurion.

00:25:37.187 --> 00:25:40.350
We don't know exactly
how the conversation went

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:43.590
but we do have what Ben
Gurion wrote in his diary

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:45.050
after the encounter.

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:46.367
Which was that and I quote it,

00:25:46.367 --> 00:25:48.630
"It is important to
make clear to our youth

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:51.530
the magnitude of the
qualitative loss resulting

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:53.990
from the extermination of 6 million

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:56.400
and therefore describe
the spiritual character

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.540
of the jewelry that was exterminated.

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:01.320
Present outstanding
personalities, like Einstein,

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:03.160
Bialik, Dubnow et cetera."

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:06.220
And the historian Deborah Lipstadt,

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:07.130
whom I just mentioned,

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:09.710
has in that same book that Ben Gurion

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:13.080
and Hausner were deeply
anxious that Baron,

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:16.370
a professor would come across as boring.

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:18.870
And Hausner actually
tried to tell the renowned

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:21.650
scholar from Columbia
University to make sure

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:23.480
his remarks were engaging,.

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.339
Which did not go over well.

00:26:25.339 --> 00:26:29.890
Boredom as Baron said,
"Boredom is relative

00:26:29.890 --> 00:26:31.550
and very often depends on the manner

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:33.170
in which the facts are presented."

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:34.527
He snapped back.

00:26:34.527 --> 00:26:37.540
"Anyway nothing was more
boring than the testimony

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:39.710
of the Yard Vashim
representative regarding

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:42.170
the validity of
certificates and documents.

00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:43.970
And yet you accepted this testimony

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:45.950
as being essential to the trial."

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:47.297
Hausner let it go.

00:26:47.297 --> 00:26:49.610
"You will of course express your opinions

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:51.400
on historical issues as you see fit,"

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:53.530
he assured the visiting professor.

00:26:53.530 --> 00:26:55.470
Your unique scientific standing

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:58.820
and your publications
are sufficient guarantee

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:00.090
that the picture you present

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:01.823
will be accurate and enthralling.

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:06.110
When Baron finally
entered the witness box,

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:08.123
about two weeks into the trial,

00:27:08.123 --> 00:27:10.360
the judge asked him to put on the keeper

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:11.450
and to take the oath.

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.840
He obliged but then quickly
took off the keeper,

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:16.380
apologizing for his imperfect Hebrew

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.110
and delivering what was in many ways

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:21.858
one of the most important
speeches he ever gave.

00:27:21.858 --> 00:27:23.548
Baron's testimony was lengthy

00:27:23.548 --> 00:27:26.760
but certain parts of it show how he tried

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:30.680
to preserve his anti Laquamore
conception by presenting

00:27:30.680 --> 00:27:33.460
the Nazi menace as
something that was entirely

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:36.480
separate and distinct
from previous pogroms

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:38.360
or anti-Jewish persecutions.

00:27:38.360 --> 00:27:41.834
And this is Baron in the witness box.

00:27:41.834 --> 00:27:44.850
The attempt to see
Auschwitz as the culmination

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:48.177
of the long history of
antisemitism he suggested

00:27:48.177 --> 00:27:52.150
entirely missed the point.

00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:52.983
And I quote,

00:27:52.983 --> 00:27:55.180
"Such assertions malign the middle ages."

00:27:55.180 --> 00:27:56.957
He said while on the stand,

00:27:56.957 --> 00:27:59.900
"Although there has been
immemorial antisemitism,

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:02.090
it is well to remember
that there was practically

00:28:02.090 --> 00:28:04.610
no violence accompanied
by blood shed under

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:06.090
the Persians of the Greeks.

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:07.390
There were minor outbreaks

00:28:08.336 --> 00:28:09.902
in the days of Philo of Alexandria.

00:28:09.902 --> 00:28:11.900
There were no pogroms under the Muslims."

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:14.330
But in keeping with both
his earlier scholarship

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:16.550
and his work in Offenbach salvaging

00:28:16.550 --> 00:28:18.450
the remnants of Jewish culture,

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:20.502
Baron also used his testimony to offer

00:28:20.502 --> 00:28:23.948
most of all an impassioned
defense of the diaspora

00:28:23.948 --> 00:28:26.000
and that it had achieved.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:31.000
And here I quote a passage
from his own remarks.

00:28:32.017 --> 00:28:35.087
"As a permanent minority
for some 2000 years,

00:28:35.087 --> 00:28:38.040
Jews were forced to seek
the kinds of openings

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.970
that were available to newcomers.

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:42.270
When they found and
used such opportunities,

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:44.530
they were working for
both their own benefit

00:28:44.530 --> 00:28:46.530
and that society as a whole.

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:48.950
I have long believed that
much of Jewish history

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:50.960
ought to be rewritten in terms of

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:53.710
the pioneering services
which the Jews were forced

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:57.187
to render by the particular
circumstances of their history."

00:28:58.385 --> 00:28:59.218
And he continued,

00:28:59.218 --> 00:29:00.690
"With courage and perseverance,

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:02.620
the Jewish people tried to adjust

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:04.900
the new situation not merely passively,

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:07.210
but independently and creatively.

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:08.910
Accustomed through the long history

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:11.720
of their dispersion to
such creative adjustments,

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.390
they were able to develop
during the interwar period,

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:17.760
certain new forms of
communal and cultural living,

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:19.986
which factified Jewish
life throughout the world,

00:29:19.986 --> 00:29:23.290
contributed significantly
to human civilization

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:25.920
and held out great promise for the future.

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:28.500
All this was cut short
by the Nazi attack,

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:30.200
unprecedented in scope,

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:33.410
geographic extension
and murderous intensity.

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:35.080
And in another moment in his remarks,

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:36.377
he said the following,

00:29:36.377 --> 00:29:37.210
"Of course,

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:39.900
the Jewish people also had
its sinners and idiots,

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.540
thieves and lunatics, but on balance,

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:45.340
future historians are likely to call

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:47.070
the first third of the 20th century,

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:49.960
the golden age of
AshkeNatziv Jewry in Europe,

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:51.130
just as they will see in it

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:53.353
the beginning of a modern
Sotheby's Renaissance.

00:29:54.940 --> 00:29:57.355
The Israelis were upset
with this testimony.

00:29:57.355 --> 00:29:59.930
Again, we have mostly just Ben Gurion's,

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:03.267
which was unsparing in
its criticism of Baron.

00:30:03.267 --> 00:30:05.377
"Just as I feared," Ben Urian wrote,

00:30:05.377 --> 00:30:07.500
"We had failed miserably."

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:09.950
Adding that, "Baron
embarrassed us deeply."

00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:12.760
The precise reasons for
that opinion are unclear

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:15.310
but the historian Hanna
Yablonka has suggested

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:18.360
that it was likely that
Baron had merely contradicted

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:20.740
what was becoming the
official Israeli narrative

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:22.220
about the Holocaust.

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:23.860
Namely that it had marked the end

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:26.080
of European Jewish civilization,

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:28.520
which had been doomed
for centuries anyway.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:31.133
But Baron's convictions
could not be shaken.

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:33.860
In conclusion,

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:36.190
I think it's worth
revisiting Baron's conception

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:38.750
of Jewish survival as he aged.

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:42.940
So in 1963, the year
retirement from Columbia,

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:44.780
he published an essay entitled

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:47.020
Newer Emphasis in Jewish History.

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:49.040
which was a revision of an earlier essay.

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.930
He had written on the
same topic years before.

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:55.530
And this is a text in
which he revisited his

00:30:55.530 --> 00:30:59.003
criticism of the Laquamore
conception of Jewish history.

00:30:59.947 --> 00:31:01.617
"All my life." He wrote,

00:31:01.617 --> 00:31:03.560
"I have been struggling against the Hitler

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:06.870
two dominant Laquamore
conception of Jewish history,

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:10.100
a term which I have been
using for more than 40 years.

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:12.110
Because I have felt that an over-emphasis

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:14.900
on Jewish sufferings
distorted the total picture

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:16.840
of the Jewish historic evolution

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:20.640
and at the same time
badly served a generation

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:22.750
which had become impatient
with the nightmare

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.410
of endless persecutions and massacres."

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:28.570
That line is often cited
but what follows is not,

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:31.310
which is Baron actively grappling with

00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:33.880
the extent of the Holocaust devastation.

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:35.097
Insisting that and I quote,

00:31:35.097 --> 00:31:37.920
"We must not misunderstand
the true realities

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:40.480
of life and psychology among those who had

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:42.837
endured the recent catastrophe including

00:31:42.837 --> 00:31:45.670
the inherent tragedies
of Jewish life during

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:47.990
the two millennia of the dispersion."

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:50.550
This I think points to
an important dimension

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:54.234
of his opposition to the
Laquamore Theory as he aged.

00:31:54.234 --> 00:31:57.120
It comes across as though he doesn't

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:58.910
have a firm answer anymore.

00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:03.343
And that perhaps the anti
Laquamore critique was never meant

00:32:03.343 --> 00:32:05.770
as an explanation for everything

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:08.010
that could be applied at will.

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:09.110
As we've seen,

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:11.050
it was an active choice that was

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:13.360
neither self evident nor inevitable.

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:16.510
And one with whom many
others certainly disagreed.

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:20.243
Although today it has gained
quite a dominant position.

00:32:20.243 --> 00:32:22.710
But perhaps most of all,

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:25.120
for someone who had lost so much,

00:32:25.120 --> 00:32:26.830
this way of writing and thinking

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:30.360
the Jewish past was
itself a form of survival.

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:33.590
Not collective survival
but personal survival.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:36.762
And that is the conclusion of my talk.

00:32:36.762 --> 00:32:39.762
(audience clapping)

00:32:43.860 --> 00:32:44.693
- Thanks, Jake.

00:32:44.693 --> 00:32:46.260
We'll take some questions.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:47.973
- Yes, with pleasure.

00:32:58.684 --> 00:32:59.517
- Thank you, Mc.

00:32:59.517 --> 00:33:00.480
Thank you. That was very moving.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:03.123
Can you say something
about his wife, Jean?

00:33:04.414 --> 00:33:05.290
I believe (mumbles)

00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:06.123
- Jeannette.

00:33:06.123 --> 00:33:07.400
- Jeannette yeah.

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:09.690
My recollection is that she was a sort

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:12.186
of full-time secretary---
- Absolutely.

00:33:12.186 --> 00:33:16.840
And research assistant
but never appeared on

00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:18.680
the title page any of his words.

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:19.730
- Absolutely.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:23.880
I believe his wife was
absolutely influential

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:26.950
on his work and more
than just as a secretary,

00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:29.434
as a real collaborator and in many ways

00:33:29.434 --> 00:33:32.730
a social and religious history of the Jews

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:35.417
is work that they did together.

00:33:35.417 --> 00:33:36.760
And you're right.

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:38.440
That she does not appear on the title page

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:40.016
of the books but it's...

00:33:40.016 --> 00:33:43.070
From everything we know about how the book

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:45.327
was produced and how it was written,

00:33:45.327 --> 00:33:47.210
especially as it was
revised in later edition,

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:48.080
she was hugely,

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.813
hugely influential on
how it was put together.

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:01.620
- Thank you for that Jake.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:04.490
Could you say something
about what motivated

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:06.799
you to take on this project?

00:34:06.799 --> 00:34:10.063
- Oh, well (laughs)how
much time do we have.

00:34:10.063 --> 00:34:13.066
(audience laughing)

00:34:13.066 --> 00:34:18.066
I guess I've been interested
in these questions

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:21.880
for a very long time particularly

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:25.509
as an American Jew who grew up.

00:34:25.509 --> 00:34:27.490
I was born in 1989,

00:34:27.490 --> 00:34:30.230
sort of right before the
fall of the Berlin wall

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:35.100
in which at no point in my education was

00:34:35.100 --> 00:34:39.140
the Holocaust not a kind
of major cornerstone.

00:34:39.140 --> 00:34:40.080
So, you know,

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:41.110
that had a history,

00:34:41.110 --> 00:34:44.200
that had a whole sort
of literature behind it.

00:34:44.200 --> 00:34:47.030
And I just became very
interested as indeed

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:49.818
so many others have in how we arrived

00:34:49.818 --> 00:34:52.850
at this conception that we
know so much about today,

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:54.070
which is the Holocaust.

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:57.300
Which might have assumed and
did assume different forms

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:59.000
and different cultural contexts.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:02.215
And so looking at the
immediate postwar with

00:35:02.215 --> 00:35:05.380
these characters, with these sort

00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:07.839
of active debates raging in that time was

00:35:07.839 --> 00:35:11.070
very interesting to me to
see sort of the choices

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:13.360
that were made and how we came to remember

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:14.710
what we came to remember

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:17.730
and like how they emerge as they did

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:19.540
but also what might have been.

00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:20.720
Because there were,

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:23.220
as we all know, many other paths,

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:25.090
whole memory project could have taken.

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:27.010
And so I find it quite interesting

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:29.053
how it did evolve as it has.

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:41.117
- Thank you.

00:35:41.117 --> 00:35:42.950
And that was a wonderful talk.

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:46.310
I'm really compelled by
your approach of thinking

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:49.560
of him as a historical actor
and not just a historian.

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:51.700
I would just love to hear
you speak more about that.

00:35:51.700 --> 00:35:54.350
How that methodology came up for you.

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:55.183
- Yeah.

00:35:55.183 --> 00:35:56.430
- 'Coz I just find it really compelling.

00:35:56.430 --> 00:35:57.895
- Ah, well.

00:35:57.895 --> 00:35:59.460
Thank you so much for that question.

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:04.460
I'm not sure it's super
complicated or anything.

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:07.950
I mean, it struck me that
in the aftermath of the war,

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:09.627
you have not just Baron but so many others

00:36:09.627 --> 00:36:14.627
like Hanna Arendt, Lucy
Dawidowicz, the list goes on.

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:19.800
These were all the first and
like Raul, Hillberg, et cetera.

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:23.270
These were all the first major
historians of the Holocaust.

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:24.251
I mean,

00:36:24.251 --> 00:36:25.270
Baron not so much the
historian of the Holocaust

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:26.103
but you know,

00:36:26.103 --> 00:36:28.410
major Jewish historians
of the time grappling

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.769
with the Holocaust, I should say.

00:36:30.769 --> 00:36:35.769
And were these amazing and Baron's case,

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:40.220
just tremendously prolific scholars

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.420
but they were more than that.

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:45.060
They were, they were
active participants in...

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:47.960
I mean many of them like Baron had had

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.870
the experience of having family killed.

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:52.480
Arendt herself as a refugee.

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:55.600
Dawidowicz had been in
Vilnius before the war

00:36:55.600 --> 00:36:57.090
and had come back to
Europe to do this part.

00:36:57.090 --> 00:37:00.630
So they were not just sort
of observers of the events.

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:03.680
The events had shaped all
of their lives intimately.

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:06.010
And I just think it's a
mistake not to consider

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:07.780
both at the same time and to consider

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:10.450
the scholarship in some
senses as an expression

00:37:10.450 --> 00:37:14.420
of the personal journeys
that so many of them had.

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:17.170
Because in a way that was these sort

00:37:17.170 --> 00:37:19.970
of emotional drive behind so much of it

00:37:19.970 --> 00:37:21.193
or so it seems to me.

00:37:22.092 --> 00:37:22.925
- Thank you for that.

00:37:22.925 --> 00:37:23.758
- Yeah.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:33.703
- Thank you, Jake.

00:37:33.703 --> 00:37:38.703
That was elegant analysis of Salo Baron.

00:37:39.087 --> 00:37:44.087
In terms of the impact of
the Holocaust on Baron.

00:37:44.138 --> 00:37:48.900
I think that the whole
question of legal status

00:37:48.900 --> 00:37:52.610
and guarantee of rights,
it was central for him.

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:57.610
after all Baron was
stateless for over 10 years.

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:02.210
It was only when Wise invited him to come

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:04.750
to New York to the Jewish
Institute of Religion

00:38:04.750 --> 00:38:08.700
that Wise was able to
flex his muscles with

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:12.143
the state department
and to get Baron a Visa

00:38:12.143 --> 00:38:14.303
and US citizenship.

00:38:15.275 --> 00:38:20.275
But in his later. in his
essays from the 1960s,

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:26.040
which I think reflected his own experience

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:28.080
of statelessness with the collapse of

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:31.410
the a Austria-Hungarian monarchy,

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:36.410
he wrote about the insufficiency
of the nation state

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:40.200
as guaranteeing the rights of Jews

00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:43.360
and other minorities and looked forward to

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:45.410
there being some kind of organization.

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:47.310
Of course the United Nations existed,

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:49.230
the League of Nations had failed at that,

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:52.720
but some kind of
international organization

00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:56.340
which would which would
guarantee basic rights.

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:57.173
So, you know,

00:38:57.173 --> 00:38:58.650
I'm wondering if you could
just comment on that.

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:00.020
- Yeah.

00:39:00.020 --> 00:39:01.440
I mean, I think that's certainly...

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:02.460
I mean, again, you know,

00:39:02.460 --> 00:39:03.960
this way, way better than I do.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.110
So, that's absolutely
something I will think

00:39:06.110 --> 00:39:07.810
more about as I continue my research.

00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:11.140
But that in terms of what
at least I have thought

00:39:11.140 --> 00:39:12.361
about so far,

00:39:12.361 --> 00:39:15.890
I think you absolutely to get that sense

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:18.970
in what he wrote about Nazism
sort of even at the time.

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:21.050
I even quote one of the
passages where he says

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:23.820
that it is this sort of national character

00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:26.010
of the enterprise that's
so hostile to the Jews.

00:39:26.010 --> 00:39:26.900
And in fact,

00:39:26.900 --> 00:39:30.980
if Nazi Germany had become
this sort of empire of sorts,

00:39:30.980 --> 00:39:33.960
that was multinational,
multilingual, et cetera

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:35.810
then perhaps things might have been wise.

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:38.123
So I think you're absolutely right.

00:39:38.123 --> 00:39:38.956
I mean,

00:39:38.956 --> 00:39:42.310
as for what he imagined in
the long term in the postwar,

00:39:42.310 --> 00:39:46.530
I don't know as much, but I don't know.

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:51.530
I, I think the emphasis on these kind

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:55.060
of supernational
organizations would put him in

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.810
the context of many other
thinkers of his time.

00:39:57.810 --> 00:39:58.643
So, I mean,

00:39:58.643 --> 00:40:00.634
it's not necessarily unique in that regard

00:40:00.634 --> 00:40:04.533
but that's a really good
point to examine further.

00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:13.368
- Thank you.

00:40:13.368 --> 00:40:14.590
This has been enjoyable and moving

00:40:14.590 --> 00:40:16.570
and I wanna ask you whether there's...

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:19.220
It felt like there was
an intimation of a kind

00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:22.070
of anti-Jewish nationalist trajectory.

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:25.600
First from the letters
creating Baron's post

00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:29.000
and then in kind of his conflict explicit

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:31.330
or implicit with Ben Gurion at the trial.

00:40:31.330 --> 00:40:33.910
And I'm (indistinct) you've
made kind of no reference

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:36.660
to how the creation of state of Israel did

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:38.710
or didn't shape bar picture.

00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:40.260
And I'm not asking you to speak

00:40:41.191 --> 00:40:43.329
to kind of the present but
in terms of thinking about,

00:40:43.329 --> 00:40:45.226
is this emphasis on spiritual status,

00:40:45.226 --> 00:40:48.010
which he's how he
referred to European jury,

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:49.930
a kind of a consistent through line

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:52.380
that did in fact place him at odds with

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:54.460
a more political or nationalist

00:40:54.460 --> 00:40:55.710
orientation towards Jewishness.

00:40:55.710 --> 00:40:56.977
- So that is an excellent question.

00:40:56.977 --> 00:40:58.760
And I'm very glad you raised that.

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:00.740
I think if I had more time,

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:03.680
I would've sort of delved
into that aspect of his work.

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:04.831
So, but first of all,

00:41:04.831 --> 00:41:07.212
you know, Baron was a Zionist, absolutely.

00:41:07.212 --> 00:41:11.170
I think the perceived conflict,

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:12.940
which I'm not sure was an actual conflict

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:17.840
with Ben Gurion at the
end was perhaps over

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:20.880
what kind of Zionism them each imagine.

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:24.020
So I don't think that
Baron was in any way,

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.880
anything but supportive
of the establishment.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:29.950
I mean, he had visited
several times before Eichmann.

00:41:29.950 --> 00:41:33.930
It wasn't something that
he opposed by any means

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:36.840
and he was a sort of
outspoken supporter of it.

00:41:36.840 --> 00:41:41.840
I just think that he did
not necessarily agree

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:45.490
with the I guess official
narrative at the time,

00:41:45.490 --> 00:41:48.020
which was to sort of
disparage the diaspora

00:41:48.020 --> 00:41:50.270
as worthless, as doomed to fail,

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:53.910
as something that is nothing but

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:55.440
the anti chamber to Auschwitz.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:58.200
And I think that that was the disagreement

00:41:58.200 --> 00:41:59.140
if ever there were one.

00:41:59.140 --> 00:42:03.203
I mean he was absolutely
a Zionist for sure.

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:07.307
- Can I get a second?

00:42:07.307 --> 00:42:08.140
- Correct.
(laughing)

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:08.973
- Yeah, of course.

00:42:08.973 --> 00:42:09.806
- You allude to him.

00:42:09.806 --> 00:42:13.805
And I think this follows
up on the previous question

00:42:13.805 --> 00:42:14.960
and that is there seems to be a narrative

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:17.140
that could be spelled out
in terms of his relation

00:42:17.140 --> 00:42:20.216
to Schlom to towering--

00:42:20.216 --> 00:42:21.490
- Yes.

00:42:21.490 --> 00:42:24.820
- Intellectual academics but
are very different sorts.

00:42:24.820 --> 00:42:25.653
And, and of course...

00:42:25.653 --> 00:42:27.190
- And on and on--

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:29.417
- Schlom was also involved in reclamation

00:42:29.417 --> 00:42:31.180
of books from after the Holocaust.

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:35.010
So in some ways there are
unparallel path switch.

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:35.843
- Absolutely.

00:42:35.843 --> 00:42:38.210
- Don't don't intersect or maybe intersect

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:40.730
more than we've been able to see.

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:41.563
- I mean,

00:42:41.563 --> 00:42:43.891
I think one of the things
that's been so interesting

00:42:43.891 --> 00:42:46.100
for me about this whole project is just

00:42:46.100 --> 00:42:49.900
the sort of interpersonal
relationships among,

00:42:49.900 --> 00:42:50.733
as you say,

00:42:50.733 --> 00:42:53.000
so many of these towering
figures that we all know

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:56.789
such as Schlom, Baron,
but also Hannah Arendt.

00:42:56.789 --> 00:42:59.780
I was fascinated to learn that...

00:42:59.780 --> 00:43:03.507
I think Baron shared in
the criticism of Eichman

00:43:03.507 --> 00:43:07.470
and Jerusalem but ultimately
was very close with RN.

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:08.303
I mean, in fact,

00:43:08.303 --> 00:43:10.450
I believe it was the Baron's who were

00:43:10.450 --> 00:43:13.390
in Hannah Arendt's apartment when she died

00:43:13.390 --> 00:43:15.570
and who called the ambulance
ones to collect her.

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:18.250
So there was this sort of intimacy among

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:19.840
this whole generation that went through

00:43:19.840 --> 00:43:22.820
these events together
even if they disagreed.

00:43:22.820 --> 00:43:24.950
So, one of the things I'm
hoping to do in the book

00:43:24.950 --> 00:43:26.920
is to tease out as much as possible

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:29.680
some of those personal
relationships that stitch

00:43:29.680 --> 00:43:31.869
these characters together because I think

00:43:31.869 --> 00:43:36.869
it's quite illuminating and
it shows the way that the...

00:43:37.700 --> 00:43:39.060
Well, I mean,

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:40.293
as we all know from people who live in

00:43:40.293 --> 00:43:41.126
the real world but disagreements

00:43:41.126 --> 00:43:42.540
between brilliant people are not always

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:43.700
intellectual in nature.

00:43:43.700 --> 00:43:47.000
That there were sort of petty personal

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:47.837
elements of it as well.

00:43:47.837 --> 00:43:50.120
And I think that that's equally important

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:53.040
in understanding how these ideas

00:43:53.040 --> 00:43:57.680
and how these arguments
get mobilized and defended.

00:43:57.680 --> 00:43:59.473
So I absolutely agree.

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:08.570
- Jake,

00:44:08.570 --> 00:44:11.750
this was just really so fascinating

00:44:11.750 --> 00:44:14.893
and obviously is just
in part of what you're

00:44:14.893 --> 00:44:19.040
gonna be creating in this next book.

00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:21.260
Can you give us a preview of, I mean,

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:23.940
you have just started to give us a preview

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:26.010
of the other aspects
that you're gonna break

00:44:26.010 --> 00:44:27.670
the other people and the other aspects.

00:44:27.670 --> 00:44:29.660
Can you fill that out even more?

00:44:29.660 --> 00:44:31.230
I obviously this is.
- Sure.

00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:32.589
- Early on, I'm just curious.

00:44:32.589 --> 00:44:35.770
- It's sort of being planned as we speak

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:40.260
but basically I have several characters,

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:45.260
one of which is Baron
but also Hannah Arendt,

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:49.080
Lucy Dawidowicz, Raul Hilberg

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:51.054
and Elie Wiesel for the moment.

00:44:51.054 --> 00:44:52.430
And looking at the...

00:44:52.430 --> 00:44:55.290
I mean, I should say, as we all know,

00:44:55.290 --> 00:44:58.083
it is an enormous huge amount of material

00:44:58.083 --> 00:45:01.850
but the way that I'm
trying to sort of find

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:04.410
my way through is to follow the characters

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:06.080
at moments when they all reappear.

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:07.920
And again, as I mentioned
they all knew each other

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:10.460
and were largely in contact in many cases

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:11.530
for their entire lives.

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:13.590
So one of those moments would be Offenbach

00:45:13.590 --> 00:45:15.780
when so many of the
characters work together.

00:45:15.780 --> 00:45:17.970
Another is the Eichmann Trial when a lot of

00:45:17.970 --> 00:45:21.000
them have cameo appearances
in different ways.

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:23.970
And then again, in New York, in the 1970s,

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:28.970
when so many are kind of
debating like Lucy Dawidowicz

00:45:29.187 --> 00:45:33.990
and Hilberg for instance, the
meaning of it all.

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:37.160
And so that's how I've structured it.

00:45:37.160 --> 00:45:42.160
And that it will come
together but we shall see.

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:46.420
- Really. I had a very similar question.

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:47.253
Yeah.

00:45:47.253 --> 00:45:49.700
But an angle on the
question, which you know,

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:52.090
you were talking about the
construction of a memory.

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:56.220
So I wonder when does it begin for you?

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:59.123
And I guess that's a
question of imagination.

00:45:59.123 --> 00:46:00.210
- When does what begin?

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:01.760
- The memory of the Holocaust?
- Yeah.

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:04.641
- When does it begin
or when does the story

00:46:04.641 --> 00:46:06.077
that you're telling begin?
- Yeah.

00:46:06.077 --> 00:46:09.680
- That is another excellent question.

00:46:09.680 --> 00:46:14.680
I think the question of when it begins has

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:17.409
to be before the war is even over.

00:46:17.409 --> 00:46:19.230
Absolutely.

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:24.230
And I think that there are
several important examples,

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:28.480
but one of them would be with the story

00:46:28.480 --> 00:46:30.490
of Emmanuel Ringelblum
and the Warsaw Ghetto

00:46:30.490 --> 00:46:33.810
sort of actively collecting trace of

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:35.760
the destruction while it was happening

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:37.750
and shoving them underground to make sure

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:39.440
that someone someday,

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:41.550
somehow would find them and know.

00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:46.190
Now, that's not necessarily telling people

00:46:46.190 --> 00:46:49.020
what the memory should be
and arbitrating the shape

00:46:49.020 --> 00:46:50.440
and contours of it,

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:53.980
but it is providing raw
sort of primary sources

00:46:53.980 --> 00:46:57.410
if you will for someone
else to construct it.

00:46:57.410 --> 00:46:59.720
But it does show a consciousness of

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:01.380
the world historical significance of

00:47:01.380 --> 00:47:03.620
the event while it was
unfolding and the imperative

00:47:03.620 --> 00:47:06.740
to have some basis to remember it.

00:47:06.740 --> 00:47:08.970
So I would say it definitely begins

00:47:11.698 --> 00:47:14.669
while the war is still raging.

00:47:14.669 --> 00:47:19.669
But then also I think
some of the most important

00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:23.370
postwar beginnings of the memory question

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:25.669
were the question of names, right?

00:47:25.669 --> 00:47:29.270
And I mentioned that
at the very beginning,

00:47:29.270 --> 00:47:31.010
like for scholars like Baron,

00:47:31.010 --> 00:47:33.720
what Baron initially called
the great catastrophe,

00:47:33.720 --> 00:47:34.990
had no official name.

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:37.290
You know, many used
different names describe it.

00:47:37.290 --> 00:47:39.720
And what's fascinating to observe

00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:43.120
and to look at is the evolution of each of

00:47:43.120 --> 00:47:46.360
the terms offered to
describe what we now call

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:49.410
the Holocaust in English but
which is called the Showa

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:51.176
and Hebrew and French.

00:47:51.176 --> 00:47:54.810
How the terms emerged,

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:56.270
what their antecedence were,

00:47:56.270 --> 00:48:00.120
what kind of implications
they gave the event

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:02.730
and how it shaped public
understandings of them.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:03.860
So, I think that that's probably

00:48:03.860 --> 00:48:07.003
the next really crucial step. Yeah.

00:48:12.477 --> 00:48:13.891
(indistinct)

00:48:13.891 --> 00:48:14.724
Alright.

00:48:14.724 --> 00:48:17.318
- Continue informally but
before that (indistinct)

00:48:17.318 --> 00:48:19.376
- Well, thank you all for coming.

00:48:19.376 --> 00:48:20.243
(audience clapping)
So much.

